  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A return to dry and seasonable weather this\nweekend.\n\nSeasonable temperatures are expected through the weekend along\nwith dry conditions. NW winds will gust as high as 30 to 35 mph\nin the wake of the frontal passage late yesterday evening,\nbefore diminishing to 15 mph or less tonight. \n\nOn Sunday, shortwave ridging will build over the area, while at\nthe surface, a lee-side trof develops and pushes east of the\narea during the evening. It will be a few degs warmer owing to\nhigher heights and westerly downslope flow, but with noticeable\nless wind. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather continues next week before a colder and more \nunsettled pattern ensues by Friday.\n\nAmplified flow within the northern stream will allow for a series of \ndry cold frontal passages across the area next week. After the first \nboundary pushes through late in the weekend, an additional system \nraces through on Tuesday. These are moisture starved air masses so \nthe only notable changes will be an increase in wind speeds and the \nreinforcement of drier air. Through the middle of the week, skies \nshould largely be clear which would support abundant sunshine. Daily \nhigh temperatures will mainly be in the 60s, with 50s across the \nmountains. One exception would be on Tuesday which is the mildest \nday of the period as highs return to the low 70s. Nighttime \nconditions will be seasonably chilly owing to the dry air and lack \nof cloud cover. Most nights should favor low temperatures in the mid \n30s to mid 40s, but with localized pockets of upper 20s to low 30s \nacross sheltered mountain and river valeys. This may lead to some \nfrost/freeze concerns where the growing season continues (most spots \neast of the Blue Ridge).\n\nToward the latter half of the next work week, global models continue \nto advertise a broad deepening trough approaching from the Upper \nMidwest. This eventually evolves into more of an omega block setup \nwith building heights over the western states and troughing across \nthe eastern Pacific. The global ensemble box-and-whisker plots show \na decided drop in temperatures. Cold temperatures aloft underneath \nthis upper low could even spread some rain or snow showers over the \nAlleghenies on Friday. Uncertainty remains high though, but a \ncooling trend certainly looks likely heading into Friday and next \nweekend.",
